 need produce full content of src/nbody.c. We'll write code accordingly.\n\nLet's design:\n\n- Define N_ITER, DT, SOFTENING same.\n- Instead of Body struct, use separate arrays: float *x,*y,*z,*vx,*vy,*vz; allocate with malloc(n*sizeof(float)).\n- read_dataset reads nbodies then reads n*6 floats? The input binary format is six float per body in order x,y,z,vx,vy,vz. We can read into a temporary buffer or directly into arrays. Since fread expects contiguous memory for each array separately; we need to read all at once into a single block and then split? Or read sequentially: first read nbodies, then allocate six arrays, then loop reading 6 floats per body using fread of one Body struct? But input is exactly sequence of 6 floats per body. We can read the whole data as an array of float[6*n] into a temporary buffer and then copy to SoA; or we can use fread for each component separately: e.g., fread(x, sizeof(float), n, stdin); fread(y,...) etc. But input order is x,y,z,vx,vy,vz per body, so reading all x first would not match because data interleaved. So need to read into AoS buffer then transpose.\n\nSimpler: keep Body struct for I/O but internally use SoA? We can allocate a temporary Body array for reading and writing, and also allocate SoA arrays; after reading, copy from temp to SoA; compute using SoA; before write, copy back. That adds overhead but maybe still faster due to better cache in force loop. But copying O(n) per iteration? Actually only once at start and end, not per iteration. So fine.\n\nAlternatively we can read directly into a flat float array of size 6*n (interleaved), then create pointers that index with stride? That would be AoS anyway. To get SoA we need transpose.\n\nGiven n is unknown but likely large enough; copying O(n) once is negligible compared to O(n^2) force computation.\n\nWe'll implement:\n\n- read_dataset: reads nbodies, allocates Body *tmp = malloc(n*sizeof(Body)); fread(tmp,...); then allocate x,y,z,vx,vy,vz arrays of size n; copy from tmp[i].x etc. Free tmp? We can keep tmp for writing later or free and re-read? Better to store both? To avoid extra memory, we could read into a flat buffer and then transpose in place? But simpler: allocate SoA arrays, read into temporary Body array, copy, free temp. For write, we need output same format; we can either keep the original AoS data or reconstruct from SoA. We'll allocate another Body* out = malloc(n*sizeof(Body)) for writing after simulation, fill from SoA, fwrite, free.\n\nBut that adds memory and time but negligible.\n\nAlternatively we could change I/O to read directly into a flat array of 6*n floats (interleaved) and then use index arithmetic in force loop? That's AoS. To get SoA without copy, we can allocate six arrays and read each body's components one by one using fread for each float: e.g., for i in n: fread(&x[i],4,1); fread(&y[i],4,1)... but that would be many small reads; inefficient. Better to read whole block into temp.\n\nWe'll do copy approach.\n\nNow force function:\n\nvoid body_force(float *x, float *y, float *z, float *vx, float *vy, float *vz, int n, float dt) {\n  #pragma omp parallel for schedule(static)\n  for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {\n    float fx=0,fy=0,fz=0;\n    float xi=x[i], yi=y[i], zi=z[i];\n    for (int j=0;j<n;++j){\n      float dx = x[j]-xi;\n      ...\n    }\n    vx[i] += dt*fx; ...\n  }\n}\n\nWe need include <omp.h>? Not necessary if using pragmas, but we may want to set num_threads? The runtime sets OMP_NUM_THREADS=4 externally. We can just use default.\n\nPotential issue: OpenMP parallel for over i with static schedule distributes contiguous chunks; each thread reads all j arrays sequentially. That's fine.\n\nWe should also consider using restrict pointers or const? Not needed.\n\nTo preserve exact FP, we must ensure compiler does not vectorize inner loop in a way that changes order. With -O3 and no explicit SIMD, GCC may auto-vectorize if it can prove associativity? For float reduction, GCC typically doesn't