orce[i]. This processes j blocks sequentially, and within a block loops over i and j. The order of accumulation per i is still j ascending because we process blocks in increasing j order and within block j ascending. So FP results identical.\n\nThis blocking improves cache: for each j-block (size B), the coordinates x[j],y[j],z[j] are loaded once into cache, then used for all n i's. This reduces memory traffic from O(n^2) reads of body data to O(n * n/B?) Actually original reads p[j] for each i,j -> n^2 reads. Blocked: for each j-block, we read B bodies' coordinates (B*3 floats), then loop over all i and use them; so total reads = (n/B)*B*n? Wait for each block of size B, we load B bodies once, then for each i we access those B bodies from cache. So memory traffic from main memory is O(n * n)?? Let's compute: For each j-block, we read B*3 floats from memory; there are n/B blocks, so total reads = (n/B)*B*3*n? Actually for each block, we loop over all i and within that loop access the same B bodies repeatedly. If cache holds them, only first access per block goes to main memory; subsequent accesses hit cache. So main memory traffic is O(n * n)?? No: For each j-block, we load B bodies (3B floats). Then for each i (n times), we read those B bodies from cache. So total main memory loads = number of blocks * 3B = (n/B)*3B = 3n per full pass? Wait that's only if we process all i for a given block before moving to next block. Yes, so total main memory reads = n/B blocks * 3B floats = 3n floats per iteration? That seems too low; but each body is read once per block? Actually each j appears in exactly one block, and its coordinates are loaded from main memory when that block starts. Then used for all i. So total main memory loads of body data = n bodies * 3 floats = 3n per force computation (plus maybe repeated if cache evicts). That's O(n) instead of O(n^2)! This is huge improvement in memory traffic, but the compute still O(n^2). However with multiple threads, each thread 